**Read:** [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), the full desert/eolian, evaporite, and terrestrial-deposition treatments; [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), the relevant sediment, weathering, stream, and climate sections; [Discovering Geology — British Geological Survey](../sources/Discovering-Geology-BGS.lingua#r_5ZRRQ0YYJEH16ZR7172P1PE4VC), the complete relevant landscape/process pages used.

Arid landscapes reflect limited moisture, material supply, wind, episodic runoff, relief, vegetation, and long histories. Sand dunes are one component rather than a definition of every desert. Bedrock surfaces, gravel pavements, fans, channels, playas, evaporites, and dust pathways record different processes and timescales.

Wind transport selectively moves material according to conditions and grain properties. Dust can carry fine material far from its source; dune migration and internal structures require observations of geometry, sediment, and changing winds. Cross-bedding alone is not uniquely eolian, so compare scale, textures, associated deposits, and regional context.

Infrequent water events can move substantial sediment and change channels or fans. A dry channel is evidence of present conditions, not proof that water is geologically unimportant. Evaporite formation requires a water/solute budget and saturation history; dryness by itself does not identify every mineral or deposit.

**Work:** compare an eolian, alluvial-fan, and playa example from complete published descriptions. Construct source/transport/storage accounts and identify features distinguishing wind deposition from water reworking. Reconstruct what a deposit can and cannot establish about past moisture or atmospheric circulation.

Use the sedimentary, weathering, stream, and climate readings together. Connect these materials with dust, groundwater, construction ground, and evaporitic resources. The regional and resource sequences will reuse the environmental reasoning without replacing detailed sedimentology or economic-geology work.

**Continue:** [Interpret climate history through mechanisms, proxies, and chronological evidence](../history/Climate-carbon-and-geological-proxies.lingua#r_2JEZDTTEKAS0C4ZY0CEB1T5V4T).

**Return:** [Geology — Earth materials, processes, history, and investigation](../Geology.lingua#r_3CK43VQS79NK0STWNPSCS8YM06).
